CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ f\(T\*\) is more specialized than f\(const U&\): SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*does not uniquely resolve.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.func.order] partial ordering as a tie-breaker in overload
resolution.  For an lvalue `int*` argument, f(T*) and f(const U&) have
indistinguishable conversion sequences (both exact-match), so the call is
resolved by partial ordering, under which f(T*) is more specialized (deducing U
from a synthesized X* succeeds; deducing T* from a synthesized Y fails).

disambiguate_functions now runs deduction-based partial ordering
([temp.deduct.partial]) across function-template candidates that tie on
conversion distance, via function_template_at_least_as_specialised.  This is the
header-free analogue of the std::to_address(_Tp*) vs __to_address(const _Ptr&)
ambiguity that previously blocked std::span's element access (cpp20_span).
